{"custom_id":"spider__color__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6jk3SJ51ZSHKVxfotnr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F36CDz3qab1D7PcJcWWrXL","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"In the big front eyes of the jumping spider Plexippus, cells sense ultraviolet and green only, and another jumping spider, Evarcha, picked orange or blue stripes over plain surfaces of every shade of gray it was offered. In two garden spiders, Argiope bruennichi and Argiope amoena, the front eyes have three kinds of color-sensing cell, for ultraviolet, blue and green. Nobody has measured which kinds of color-sensing cell you have, or whether you can tell colors apart.","cites":["R2","R3","R4"],"note":"Left out the count of gray shades and the wavelength figures, since neither has a job in the row."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3414,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":756,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":458},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"cat__sharpness__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6jSaTbtDY5vuVwFWeDH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XG2GV77e2b2119tLR5NmFC","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"If you are a domestic cat, you read about 10 cycles a degree, a third of the 30 that a human reads on an eye chart. If you are a cheetah, you read about 25. If you are a wildcat of the same genus as the domestic cat, such as a sand cat, you very likely see about as sharply as it does, but for the other cats nobody has measured how sharply you see.","cites":["R1","R2"],"note":"Cheetah figure rounded from 23 to 25. Left out R3 (qualitative only) and R4 (Lynx lynx estimate with no stated method)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1794,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":975,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":707},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"spider__sharpness__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6jiZJqZWEoDKXd5vRm1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013gg4X1JLdmJK885s9jBDRR","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"Nobody has measured how sharply you see. The few spiders that have been measured differ widely: the big front eyes of a Portia jumping spider read about 12 cycles a degree, where a human reads about 30, while the large rear middle eyes of the net-casting spider Dinopis read about a third of a cycle a degree. Neither figure can be carried over to you.","cites":["R2","R5"],"note":"No record covers any listed species or its genus, so no figure is carried; the row names only the measured spiders."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2325,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":862,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":618},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"dog__sharpest-spot__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6jidg24W6tTWwRDYfoX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NhG3qmPLTm1Cya3cqQ78W4","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"If you are a domestic dog with a long skull, as in a wolf, you see most sharply along a band level with the horizon, and if your skull is short, like a pug's, you see most sharply in one spot in the middle. If you are a red fox or an Arctic fox, you have one spot of sharpest sight and a streak of sharp sight running beside it. Nobody has measured this in coyotes, jackals or the other wild relatives, so nobody knows which pattern you have.","cites":["R1","R2","R3"],"note":"Left out the ganglion and cone density figures because they stay with the red fox and Arctic fox and have no comparison job here."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1709,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":793,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":505},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
